# ☕ Introduction to Java

> **Java** is a high-level, object-oriented programming language developed by **Sun Microsystems** (now owned by Oracle).

Java is not just a language — it is a **complete platform** that includes:

* Programming language
* Runtime environment (JVM)
* Libraries and APIs

---

## 🌍 Where Java is Used

Java is used across a wide range of platforms:

* 📱 Mobile Applications (Android)
* 🌐 Web Applications (Backend Development)
* 🏢 Enterprise Systems (Banking, ERP, etc.)
* 💻 Desktop Applications
* ⚙️ Embedded Systems
* ☁️ Cloud & Distributed Systems

👉 Java follows the principle:
**“Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA) : WORA is Java's core design philosophy, meaning Java code written on one platform can run on any platform without modification.
                                     How It Works
                                     The magic behind WORA is the Java Virtual Machine (JVM):
                                     Your Java Code (.java)
                                             ↓  [Java Compiler - javac]
                                       Bytecode (.class)
                                             ↓  [JVM translates for each platform]
                                        ┌────┴────┐
                                     Windows   macOS   Linux   Android”**
This is possible because of the **Java Virtual Machine (JVM)**.

---

## ⚙️ Java Compilation Process

1. Write code in `.java` file
2. Compile using Java Compiler → converts to **bytecode (.class)**
3. JVM executes the bytecode on any platform

---

## 🧠 Memory Management in Java

* Java uses **automatic memory management**
* Objects are created in **Heap Memory : Heap memory is the runtime data area where all objects and
instance variables are stored in Java. It is created when the JVM starts and is shared across all threads.**

* Variables store **references**, not actual objects
* Memory is cleaned by:

  > ♻️ **Garbage Collector (GC)**

👉 No need to manually free memory (unlike C/C++)

---

## 🔤 Java Syntax

* Java syntax is inspired by **C and C++**
* But removes complex features like:

  * ❌ Pointers
  * ❌ Manual memory management

👉 Result:

* Safer
* Easier to maintain
* More secure

---

## 🔄 Other Languages on JVM

Although Java is primary, JVM supports other languages like:

* Python (Jython)
* JavaScript (Nashorn - older)
* Ruby (JRuby)
* Groovy (Java-based scripting language)

